“Nordi Remitwave,” “we,” “us” and “our” refer to the Nordi Remitwave legal entity or entities that determine the purposes and means of processing your Personal Data for a particular product or jurisdiction.
The responsible entity may vary depending on:
- your country;
- your residence;
- the Service you use;
- the legal entity operating the product;
- whether a regulated Partner provides the financial service;
- the currency, payment rail or blockchain network involved;
- applicable licensing requirements; and
- the role of a Partner as independent controller, joint controller or processor.
We will identify the relevant Nordi Remitwave entity during onboarding, in the applicable Product Schedule, or in a jurisdiction-specific privacy notice.

This Policy applies to Personal Data processed in connection with:
- account registration and authentication;
- KYC, KYB and beneficial-ownership verification;
- payments and remittances;
- cards;
- banking or safeguarded-funds services;
- foreign exchange;
- lending and credit;
- investment and robo-advisory services;
- Digital Asset and blockchain services;
- open banking;
- customer support;
- marketing;
- fraud prevention;
- AML/CFT/CPF and sanctions screening;
- cybersecurity;
- AI-assisted features;
- websites and mobile applications;
- APIs;
- analytics;
- regulatory reporting; and
- business or institutional accounts.
This Policy does not necessarily apply to independent third parties that process your data under their own privacy policies.

Where applicable, Nordi Remitwave seeks to process Personal Data according to principles of:
- lawfulness;
- fairness;
- transparency;
- purpose limitation;
- data minimization;
- accuracy;
- storage limitation;
- integrity and confidentiality;
- security;
- accountability;
- privacy by design;
- privacy by default; and
- proportionality.
We seek to collect only the Personal Data reasonably necessary for the relevant purpose.

Depending on the Services you use, we may collect the following categories.
6.1 Identity Data
This may include:
- full legal name;
- former names;
- username;
- date of birth;
- place of birth;
- nationality;
- citizenship;
- gender where lawfully required;
- photograph;
- signature;
- national identification number;
- passport number;
- driver's licence number;
- voter identification;
- tax identification number;
- BVN, NIN or equivalent identifiers where legally permitted;
- immigration or residency status; and
- identity-document images and metadata.
6.2 Contact Data
This may include:
- residential address;
- business address;
- mailing address;
- email address;
- telephone number; and
- emergency or authorized contact details where applicable.
6.3 Financial Data
This may include:
- bank account details;
- payment-card data;
- wallet details;
- account balances;
- transaction histories;
- merchant information;
- beneficiary information;
- payment references;
- income;
- employment;
- assets;
- liabilities;
- account statements;
- cash flow;
- source of funds;
- source of wealth;
- investment holdings; and
- credit or repayment information.
6.4 Credit Data
For credit or lending Services, we may process:
- credit scores;
- credit-bureau reports;
- repayment history;
- defaults;
- outstanding debts;
- credit inquiries;
- affordability data;
- income and expenditure information;
- loan applications;
- underwriting inputs;
- adverse-action reasons;
- collateral information; and
- credit risk indicators.
6.5 Transaction Data
This may include:
- amounts;
- currencies;
- dates;
- times;
- transaction identifiers;
- originators;
- beneficiaries;
- merchants;
- payment rails;
- bank or wallet details;
- blockchain addresses;
- smart-contract addresses;
- transaction hashes;
- transaction purpose;
- device used;
- geolocation or network indicators where permitted; and
- risk or compliance flags.
6.6 Blockchain and Digital Asset Data
Public blockchains may expose data including:
- public wallet addresses;
- transaction hashes;
- token balances;
- transaction history;
- smart-contract interactions;
- counterparties;
- network identifiers;
- staking activity;
- token transfers; and
- protocol interactions.
We may associate blockchain data with account identity information where necessary for lawful compliance, fraud prevention, asset recovery, custody or transaction processing.
6.7 KYC, KYB and Compliance Data
This may include:
- identity-verification results;
- document-authenticity results;
- liveness-check outcomes;
- beneficial owners;
- directors;
- authorized signatories;
- corporate registration information;
- politically exposed person status;
- sanctions-screening results;
- adverse media;
- source-of-funds evidence;
- source-of-wealth evidence;
- transaction-monitoring alerts;
- risk classifications;
- investigations;
- regulatory requests; and
- suspicious-activity indicators.
6.8 Biometric Data
Where permitted and necessary, we may process biometric information for:
- identity verification;
- liveness checks;
- fraud prevention;
- account security; and
- regulatory compliance.
We will seek explicit or other legally required consent where Applicable Law requires it.
We do not use biometric data for unrelated advertising purposes unless expressly disclosed and lawful.
6.9 Device and Technical Data
This may include:
- IP address;
- device identifiers;
- operating system;
- browser type;
- application version;
- device model;
- language;
- time zone;
- SIM or carrier information where lawful;
- device integrity indicators;
- jailbreak or root detection;
- session identifiers;
- login timestamps;
- cookie identifiers;
- advertising identifiers where permitted;
- crash logs;
- telemetry;
- fraud signals; and
- security events.
6.10 Location Data
We may process:
- country;
- approximate location derived from IP;
- transaction location;
- device location; and
- precise geolocation where enabled and legally permitted.
Precise location will be used only where necessary and subject to applicable permission or consent requirements.
6.11 Investment and Suitability Data
This may include:
- investment objectives;
- risk tolerance;
- time horizon;
- experience;
- knowledge;
- loss-bearing capacity;
- financial circumstances;
- investment restrictions;
- portfolio holdings; and
- suitability or appropriateness assessment results.
6.12 AI Interaction Data
Where you use AI-assisted functionality, we may process:
- prompts;
- messages;
- uploaded files;
- questions;
- generated responses;
- feedback;
- conversation metadata;
- model safety flags;
- risk classifications; and
- audit logs.
Do not submit information you are not authorized to disclose.
6.13 Communications Data
We may process:
- support tickets;
- emails;
- chat transcripts;
- recorded calls where lawful;
- complaint records;
- dispute records;
- survey responses; and
- regulatory communications.
6.14 Marketing Data
This may include:
- communication preferences;
- campaign interactions;
- referral information;
- promotion eligibility;
- website events; and
- consent records.
6.15 Professional and Business Data
For business accounts, this may include:
- job title;
- employer;
- business type;
- industry;
- ownership;
- directorship;
- corporate documents;
- trading history; and
- financial statements.

We may collect Personal Data:
7.1 Directly from you
For example when you:
- register;
- complete KYC;
- apply for credit;
- connect an account;
- make a payment;
- purchase an investment;
- transfer Digital Assets;
- use an AI assistant;
- contact support; or
- respond to an investigation.
7.2 From your devices
Through apps, browsers, APIs, SDKs, cookies, device permissions and security systems.
7.3 From Partners
Including:
- banks;
- payment processors;
- card issuers;
- card networks;
- brokers;
- custodians;
- lenders;
- investment providers;
- cryptoasset providers;
- identity-verification providers;
- open-banking providers;
- credit bureaus;
- fraud-prevention providers;
- blockchain analytics providers;
- sanctions-screening providers; and
- market-data providers.
7.4 From public sources
Including:
- government registries;
- corporate registries;
- court records;
- sanctions lists;
- PEP databases;
- regulatory publications;
- blockchain networks; and
- public professional or business information.
7.5 From third parties
Including authorized representatives, employers, merchants, counterparties, beneficiaries, referral partners and business customers.

We may process Personal Data to:
8.1 Provide Services
Including to:
- open and maintain Accounts;
- authenticate users;
- execute payments;
- process remittances;
- provide currency conversion;
- operate cards;
- maintain balances;
- facilitate investments;
- provide credit;
- process Digital Asset transactions;
- connect external financial accounts; and
- provide customer support.
8.2 Verify identity
To:
- establish identity;
- verify documents;
- perform liveness checks;
- verify beneficial ownership;
- confirm authorized representatives; and
- prevent impersonation.
8.3 Comply with financial-crime laws
To:
- conduct KYC and KYB;
- screen sanctions;
- identify PEPs;
- monitor transactions;
- assess source of funds or wealth;
- analyze blockchain activity;
- identify fraud;
- investigate suspicious activity;
- submit regulatory reports; and
- respond to lawful government requests.
8.4 Assess credit
To:
- determine eligibility;
- assess affordability;
- calculate risk;
- verify income;
- obtain credit reports;
- prevent fraud;
- set credit limits;
- price credit; and
- service or collect debt.
8.5 Provide investment services
To:
- assess suitability or appropriateness;
- process orders;
- manage portfolios;
- perform rebalancing;
- generate disclosures;
- administer corporate actions; and
- comply with investment regulations.
8.6 Operate AI Systems
To:
- provide AI-assisted support;
- summarize information;
- detect fraud;
- conduct risk analysis;
- support underwriting;
- assist compliance review;
- personalize interfaces;
- identify anomalies;
- generate explanations;
- monitor model performance; and
- improve AI safety and quality where lawfully permitted.
8.7 Security
To:
- detect account takeover;
- identify malware;
- identify compromised devices;
- prevent abuse;
- investigate cyber incidents;
- secure infrastructure;
- protect funds; and
- maintain audit trails.
8.8 Legal and regulatory compliance
To comply with:
- financial regulation;
- banking regulation;
- payments regulation;
- lending law;
- securities regulation;
- Digital Asset regulation;
- tax law;
- sanctions;
- court orders;
- record-retention requirements;
- law-enforcement requests; and
- supervisory obligations.
8.9 Improve the Services
To:
- debug software;
- conduct analytics;
- evaluate features;
- improve accessibility;
- develop products;
- monitor system reliability; and
- perform quality assurance.
8.10 Marketing
Where permitted, to:
- send product updates;
- send offers;
- personalize marketing;
- measure campaigns; and
- manage referrals.
We will obtain consent where required.
8.11 Defend legal rights
To:
- enforce agreements;
- resolve disputes;
- investigate complaints;
- recover debt;
- protect rights;
- respond to litigation; and
- preserve evidence.

Where Applicable Law requires a lawful basis, we may rely on one or more of the following.
9.1 Performance of a contract
For processing necessary to provide a Service you requested.
Examples include:
- creating an Account;
- executing payments;
- providing custody;
- making transfers;
- servicing credit; and
- fulfilling investment orders.
9.2 Legal obligation
For processing required by law.
Examples include:
- KYC;
- AML;
- sanctions;
- tax reporting;
- regulatory reporting;
- transaction recordkeeping; and
- responding to lawful orders.
9.3 Legitimate interests
Where permitted, we may process Personal Data for legitimate interests such as:
- fraud prevention;
- security;
- risk management;
- service improvement;
- network security;
- internal administration;
- legal claims;
- analytics; and
- preventing misuse.
We will assess whether those interests are overridden by your rights where required.
9.4 Consent
We may rely on consent for activities such as:
- optional marketing;
- certain cookies;
- precise geolocation;
- certain biometric processing;
- optional data connections; and
- other processing where law requires consent.
You may withdraw consent as permitted by law.
9.5 Vital interests
In exceptional circumstances, we may process information to protect someone's life or physical safety where legally permitted.
9.6 Public interest or substantial public interest
Where law recognizes such a basis for financial-crime prevention, fraud prevention, safeguarding, regulatory or other activities.

We may use automated systems or AI to support or make decisions involving:
- identity verification;
- transaction monitoring;
- fraud detection;
- sanctions screening;
- account security;
- credit underwriting;
- affordability;
- credit limits;
- investment recommendations;
- transaction restrictions;
- risk scoring; and
- product eligibility.
Depending on the jurisdiction, you may have rights to:
- receive meaningful information about the logic or principal factors involved;
- understand the significance and expected consequences;
- request human review;
- express your point of view;
- contest a decision;
- request correction of inaccurate inputs; or
- request an alternative decision process.
Where a credit decision requires legally specific adverse-action reasons, we will provide the required reasons and will not rely solely on model complexity as a substitute.

Nordi Remitwave may use certain data to test, validate, secure or improve AI Systems where lawful.
We will establish governance to distinguish:
- operational inference data;
- security logs;
- model evaluation data;
- training or fine-tuning data;
- customer-support data;
- regulated financial records; and
- data excluded from training.
We will not use customer financial data, identity documents, authentication secrets, private keys or regulated sensitive data to train general-purpose models unless there is a documented lawful basis, appropriate safeguards and any required consent.
Where external AI providers are used, contractual restrictions should govern retention, training, confidentiality, security and subprocessors.

We may share Personal Data with the following categories of recipients where lawful and necessary.
14.1 Banks and payment providers
To process:
- deposits;
- withdrawals;
- transfers;
- cards;
- remittances;
- settlement; and
- foreign exchange.
14.2 Lenders and credit providers
Where a Partner provides or funds a credit product.
14.3 Credit bureaus and reference agencies
To assess credit, verify identity, prevent fraud or report repayment performance where legally permitted.
14.4 Investment firms
Including:
- brokers;
- custodians;
- investment advisers;
- exchanges;
- settlement systems; and
- market infrastructure.
14.5 Digital Asset providers
Including:
- custodians;
- exchanges;
- wallet infrastructure providers;
- liquidity providers;
- validators;
- Travel Rule providers; and
- blockchain analytics providers.
14.6 Identity and fraud providers
For identity, document, liveness, fraud and risk verification.
14.7 Cloud and technology providers
For hosting, security, analytics, communications and infrastructure.
14.8 AI providers
Where external models or infrastructure support AI functionality, subject to applicable contractual and security controls.
14.9 Regulators and public authorities
Including:
- central banks;
- securities regulators;
- financial intelligence units;
- tax authorities;
- sanctions authorities;
- law enforcement;
- courts; and
- other competent authorities.
14.10 Professional advisers
Including lawyers, auditors, accountants, consultants and insurers.
14.11 Corporate transactions
In connection with:
- mergers;
- acquisitions;
- financing;
- restructuring;
- insolvency;
- sale of assets; or
- transfer of regulated business.
14.12 Other users or counterparties
Certain information must be transmitted to payment beneficiaries, originators, banks, wallet providers or other counterparties to complete transactions.

We may use:
- essential cookies;
- security cookies;
- session cookies;
- preference cookies;
- analytics cookies;
- SDKs;
- pixels; and
- similar technologies.
Strictly necessary technologies may operate without consent where permitted.
For non-essential technologies, we will obtain consent where Applicable Law requires it.
Our cookie notice should explain:
- provider;
- purpose;
- category;
- duration;
- whether first- or third-party; and
- how preferences can be changed.

If you connect an external account, we may receive data such as:
- account holder name;
- account identifiers;
- balances;
- transactions;
- income indicators;
- merchant information; and
- payment details.
We use this information for the purposes disclosed when you authorize the connection.
You may revoke access as permitted by the applicable open-banking framework.
Revocation does not require us to delete data we must retain by law.

Nordi Remitwave may operate globally, which may require Personal Data to be processed outside your country.
Where required, we use lawful transfer mechanisms such as:
- adequacy decisions;
- standard contractual clauses;
- approved contractual clauses;
- binding corporate rules where implemented;
- contractual safeguards;
- regulatory-approved mechanisms;
- consent where valid; or
- another recognized legal basis.
We may conduct transfer risk assessments where required.
We may apply supplementary technical or organizational measures, including:
- encryption;
- access controls;
- pseudonymization;
- localization;
- regional processing; and
- contractual restrictions.

We use technical and organizational measures proportionate to the sensitivity and risk of the data.
These may include:
- encryption in transit;
- encryption at rest;
- key management;
- tokenization;
- hashing;
- pseudonymization;
- least-privilege access;
- role-based access control;
- privileged-access management;
- multi-factor authentication;
- device security;
- secrets management;
- network segmentation;
- secure software development;
- code review;
- vulnerability management;
- penetration testing;
- monitoring;
- logging;
- anomaly detection;
- DLP controls;
- backup;
- disaster recovery;
- business continuity; and
- vendor-risk management.
No system is completely secure.

We apply additional controls to credentials and authentication secrets.
We should not store passwords in plaintext.
Where Nordi Remitwave operates custodial Digital Asset infrastructure, private-key management may use technologies such as:
- hardware security modules;
- MPC;
- multisignature;
- key sharding;
- offline or cold storage; and
- restricted signing policies.
If a product is self-custodial, Nordi Remitwave may not have access to or be able to recover your private keys or seed phrases.

We retain Personal Data only for as long as necessary for the purpose for which it was collected or as required by law.
Retention may depend on:
- AML requirements;
- financial-services recordkeeping;
- tax requirements;
- lending requirements;
- securities requirements;
- complaints;
- litigation;
- fraud prevention;
- cybersecurity;
- regulatory inquiries;
- contract limitation periods; and
- account status.
Illustrative categories may include:
Data Category	Typical Retention Approach
KYC / AML records	Required statutory period after relationship ends
Transaction records	Required financial/regulatory period
Credit records	Required lending/credit reporting period
Investment records	Required securities/investment period
Support records	Business need plus legal limitation period
Security logs	Risk-based period
Marketing consent	While active plus evidence period
AI audit logs	Risk-based and regulatory period
Blockchain mappings	As required for AML, custody, tax or legal obligations


Exact periods must be set in Nordi Remitwave's internal retention schedule.
When retention expires, data should be securely deleted, anonymized or irreversibly de-identified unless continued retention is required.

Depending on your jurisdiction, you may have rights to:
- be informed;
- access Personal Data;
- receive copies;
- correct inaccurate data;
- delete data;
- restrict processing;
- object to processing;
- withdraw consent;
- portability;
- opt out of marketing;
- opt out of sale or sharing;
- limit use or disclosure of sensitive information;
- opt out of targeted advertising;
- opt out of certain profiling;
- challenge automated decisions;
- request human review;
- lodge a complaint; and
- receive equal service without unlawful discrimination for exercising privacy rights.
These rights may be subject to exceptions.
For example, we may be unable to delete KYC records that law requires us to retain.

Where lawful, we may monitor or record customer communications for:
- quality assurance;
- training;
- fraud prevention;
- complaint handling;
- security;
- regulatory compliance; and
- evidence of transactions or instructions.
We will provide notice where required.

Financial-crime prevention may require lawful sharing of information with:
- regulated Partners;
- banks;
- payment networks;
- fraud-prevention networks;
- blockchain analytics providers;
- financial intelligence units;
- law enforcement;
- regulators; and
- sanctions authorities.
Such processing may occur without consent where another legal basis applies.

Where permitted, we may:
- request credit reports;
- share repayment performance;
- report arrears or defaults;
- verify identity;
- detect fraud; and
- correct inaccurate information.
Credit bureaus may act as independent controllers under their own notices.

We may create aggregated, anonymized or de-identified data for:
- statistics;
- analytics;
- fraud research;
- financial modeling;
- product development;
- security;
- market research; and
- service improvement.
Where data has been irreversibly anonymized so that it is no longer Personal Data under Applicable Law, this Policy may no longer apply to that data.
We will not attempt to re-identify data where law prohibits re-identification.

We may use appropriately controlled data for:
- fraud-model validation;
- credit-model monitoring;
- investment-algorithm testing;
- AI safety evaluation;
- cybersecurity research;
- transaction-monitoring tuning; and
- operational-risk analysis.
Where possible, we may use synthetic, anonymized, pseudonymized or minimized data.

Access to Personal Data should be limited to personnel with a legitimate business need.
Personnel with access may be subject to:
- confidentiality obligations;
- background screening where lawful;
- privacy training;
- security training;
- role-based permissions;
- monitoring;
- disciplinary procedures; and
- access termination when roles change.

Before engaging material vendors that process Personal Data, Nordi Remitwave should perform proportionate due diligence addressing:
- security;
- privacy;
- location of processing;
- subprocessors;
- breach history;
- data retention;
- government access;
- AI training use;
- financial stability;
- business continuity; and
- regulatory cooperation.
Contracts should contain appropriate provisions concerning:
- processing instructions;
- confidentiality;
- security;
- breach notification;
- deletion or return;
- subprocessors;
- audit;
- cross-border transfers;
- assistance with rights requests;
- regulatory cooperation; and
- restrictions on independent reuse.

We maintain processes designed to identify, investigate, contain and remediate Personal Data breaches.
Where required, we will notify:
- the relevant regulator or authority;
- affected individuals;
- financial Partners;
- law enforcement; or
- other legally required recipients.
Notification will be made within applicable statutory timeframes.

We may conduct privacy or data-protection impact assessments for processing likely to present elevated risk, including:
- biometric processing;
- large-scale financial profiling;
- credit scoring;
- high-impact automated decision-making;
- large-scale monitoring;
- sensitive geolocation;
- blockchain identity analytics;
- new AI models;
- novel financial technologies; and
- international data transfers.

Product teams should incorporate privacy into:
- product requirements;
- system architecture;
- API design;
- default settings;
- access controls;
- retention;
- analytics;
- model development;
- vendor selection; and
- incident response.
Defaults should not expose more Personal Data than necessary.

We may update this Policy when:
- products change;
- data uses change;
- vendors change;
- law changes;
- regulatory guidance changes;
- corporate structure changes; or
- new technologies are introduced.
Material changes will be notified as required by Applicable Law.
The current version will show its effective date.

Where GDPR applies, Nordi Remitwave will provide information including:
- controller identity;
- DPO contact where applicable;
- processing purposes;
- lawful bases;
- legitimate interests where relied upon;
- recipients;
- international transfers;
- retention;
- data-subject rights;
- complaint rights;
- whether provision of data is mandatory;
- automated decision-making information where required; and
- source of data where not collected directly.
Data subjects may have rights of:
- access;
- rectification;
- erasure;
- restriction;
- portability;
- objection; and
- protection concerning certain solely automated decisions.
Cross-border transfers will use recognized GDPR mechanisms.

Where UK data-protection law applies:
- Nordi Remitwave will comply with applicable UK GDPR and Data Protection Act requirements, as amended;
- privacy information will be provided clearly;
- data-subject rights will be supported;
- international transfers will use lawful UK transfer mechanisms;
- cookie and electronic-marketing requirements will be applied; and
- applicable changes arising from UK data legislation will be incorporated as required.
UK privacy representative, if applicable: [INSERT]
ICO registration number: [INSERT]

Where the CCPA/CPRA applies, California residents may have rights including:
- right to know;
- right to access;
- right to delete;
- right to correct;
- right to opt out of sale or sharing;
- right to limit certain uses of sensitive Personal Information;
- right to non-discrimination; and
- rights relating to Global Privacy Control where applicable.
A California-specific notice should disclose categories of:
- Personal Information collected;
- sources;
- business or commercial purposes;
- third parties;
- information sold or shared, if any; and
- retention criteria.
Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information: [INSERT LINK, IF REQUIRED]
Limit Use of My Sensitive Personal Information: [INSERT LINK, IF REQUIRED]

Where Nordi Remitwave or a Partner is subject to financial-privacy laws such as GLBA or equivalent state regimes:
- required privacy notices will be delivered;
- nonpublic personal information will be protected;
- required opt-out rights will be implemented;
- reuse and redisclosure restrictions will be respected;
- safeguards appropriate to the regulated entity will be maintained; and
- separate federal or state financial privacy requirements will apply where relevant.

Nordi Remitwave must maintain jurisdiction-specific controls for AI.
Where applicable:
- users will be informed when they are interacting with AI;
- AI-generated or materially manipulated content will be labeled where required;
- high-impact systems will be documented;
- decisions will remain contestable where required;
- model outputs will be monitored for bias, accuracy and drift;
- prohibited or impermissible AI practices will not be deployed; and
- human oversight will be incorporated where legally required.

Nordi Remitwave should maintain procedures to:
1. receive a request;
2. authenticate the requester;
3. identify applicable law;
4. locate relevant systems;
5. apply exemptions;
6. review third-party information;
7. export or correct data;
8. delete or restrict where required;
9. record the response;
10. meet applicable deadlines;
11. provide appeal mechanisms where required; and
12. retain evidence of compliance.

Before launch:
- [ ] map all Personal Data flows;
- [ ] identify controller/processor roles;
- [ ] complete records of processing;
- [ ] identify lawful bases;
- [ ] document legitimate-interest assessments;
- [ ] complete DPIAs for high-risk processing;
- [ ] review AI systems;
- [ ] review automated decisions;
- [ ] document credit-model inputs;
- [ ] restrict special-category/sensitive data;
- [ ] verify consent mechanisms;
- [ ] configure cookie consent;
- [ ] enable privacy-rights workflow;
- [ ] complete retention schedule;
- [ ] configure secure deletion;
- [ ] identify transfer mechanisms;
- [ ] complete vendor DPAs;
- [ ] assess subprocessors;
- [ ] implement breach response;
- [ ] establish DPO/privacy officer governance;
- [ ] review biometric requirements;
- [ ] review open-banking permissions;
- [ ] review blockchain immutability disclosures;
- [ ] establish AI training restrictions;
- [ ] implement Global Privacy Control where required;
- [ ] create California notice where applicable;
- [ ] create GLBA notice where applicable;
- [ ] create Nigeria NDPA notices where applicable;
- [ ] create EU/UK notices where applicable; and
- [ ] conduct legal review before publication.

